<article class="content">
<h1>Thigh rise and physical fit checks</h1>
<p>Base pad size locates the knee area; thigh rise changes coverage above it. A preferred “+1” or “+2” cannot replace a sourced base-size rule.</p>
<section class="card"><h2>What “+1” and “+2” mean</h2>
<p>The “+” number is extra length added at the top of the pad, above the thigh-rise break. It raises coverage toward the five-hole without changing where the knee cradle sits. Manufacturers treat it differently: CCM lists thigh rise as personal preference, Warrior sizes it in half-inch steps from a thigh measurement, Brian's pairs each stock size with a thigh-rise length, and Vaughn's stock senior pads mostly ship +2.</p>
<p>The subtlety that matters: a 33+2 and a 34+1 are the same total height, but the knee cradle sits in different places because base size — not total height — sets knee position. Pick base size from your measurement first; choose rise second.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Read extension mappings honestly</h2>
<p>In reviewed Warrior data, a blank mapping means no reviewed extension value was published. An explicit <strong>N/A</strong> means that extension is unavailable for the tuple. Neither means zero, and neither should be guessed.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Verify the knee block</h2>
<ol>
<li>Put on skates, pants and knee protection used in play. Trying pads in shoes gives a false read.</li>
<li>Strap the pad according to its instructions and settle into stance. Straps should be snug, not cranked — if a pad only feels right fully cinched, the size or model is wrong.</li>
<li>Drop into butterfly several times. The knee must land securely on the block without sliding off an edge.</li>
<li>Read the landing: knee above the block means the pad is too small; well below the block means too big. For a growing goalie, up to about one inch below the block's centre is the accepted allowance — never more.</li>
<li>Check boot angle, pad rotation and thigh-rise interference while recovering to stance. A properly sized pad rotates cleanly between stance and butterfly.</li>
</ol></section>
<section class="card"><h2>When to stop</h2>
<p>Stop and consult a qualified fitter when the chart returns a conflict, source gap, provisional tuple, or cannot-determine result; when the knee misses the block; or when model geometry changes the expected landing.</p></section>
